      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND AND PURPOSE",
          "content": "Right transradial approach (TRA) for cardiovascular procedures is becoming common practice, but angiography and PCI of left mammary artery by-pass graft (LIMA) via the right radial artery is still technically challenging. Aim of the study was to evaluate the possibility to put a stable catheter in left mammary from right radial approach."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "From June 2002 to March 2009, 246 consecutive patients (88% male; age 67.7 +/- 7.8 years) with previous CABG underwent LIMA graft angiography via right TRA by a single experienced operator."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "An adequate radial access was obtained in all patients. Selective angiography of LIMA was successfully performed in 218 patients (89.1%). In the remaining 28 (10.9%) the LIMA approach from right radial failed because of severe tortuosity of brachiocephalic trunk or severe calcified and tortuous left subclavian artery. 13 patients underwent PCI and stentingon mid-distal native LAD trough LIMA using 6FIM4 guiding catheter. No periprocedural cerebro-vascular complications, no vascular injury at the LIMA takeoff as a result of selective canulation, no injury of the left subclavian artery were observed"
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "In patients with previous CABG, the presence of LIMA grafts is not a limitation to successfully perform diagnostic and interventional procedures via the right radial approach with experienced operators."
        }
      ]},
